# shutdown does NOT halt, and that is the tape's design, not a fault.  It ends
# `exec kill 1', and init.c:60 handles SIGTERM with reset() -- a longjmp to
# the top of main (init.c:364-367) -- so the machine returns to single user.
# reboot(8) is where the rest of the sequence lives: "invoke kill 1 to take
# the system to single user, unmount file systems with /etc/umount -a and
# halt and restart the system as described below under `Console boots'",
# which is control-P then >>> H -- simh's ^E then quit.  The same page's BUGS
# say of /etc/halt and /etc/reboot that "their function is indeterminate and
# likely to change", so the console is the documented halt and not those.
# /etc/down is OURS and does what shutdown does not: it halts.  Separate name
# rather than a replacement, so the tape's shutdown stays the tape's -- `down'
# collides with nothing, being in no Admin list, no source directory and no
# manual page.  It is reboot(8)'s own tail end -- "unmount file systems with
# /etc/umount -a and halt" -- ending at /etc/halt, whose RB_HALT is the only
# one of reboot.h's six flags this kernel reads (machdep.c:443, the sole
# occurrence in lsys/).  boot() does not sync, so both syncs are userland's.
# getty.c:207 is execl("/etc/login", "login", name, 0).  login is in BOTH
# Admin/binfiles and Admin/etcfiles -- Admin/dest answers /bin because
# lookline tries binfiles first, so reading dest alone loses the second
# destination.  Without /etc/login the exec fails, getty exits 1, init
# reforks it, and the console loops on banner-and-prompt printing nothing.
$ETC/login:Psh $GEN/installed: $PREP $DEST/login
	ln $DEST/login $ETC/login || echo '/etc/login: FAILED'

